  <p class="article-answer"><strong>Project status:</strong> in progress. These field photos document a new paver patio and its surrounding grade transitions while the layout, base and paving work are still visible. We will add completed views once the final details and cleanup are finished.</p>

  <h2 id="scope">What is taking shape</h2>
  <p>The project creates a more defined outdoor room beside an existing concrete area. A low wall and stepped edge hold the change in grade, while the new paver field introduces a quieter, more finished surface for daily use.</p>
  <p>The selected pavers mix several rectangular sizes in a restrained gray palette. A darker border gives the field a clear edge without turning it into a decorative frame. That distinction matters in a space with existing masonry, planting and mature trees already competing for attention.</p>

  <h2 id="base">The work that disappears below the patio</h2>
  <p>One photo captures the project before most of the pavers were installed. The open area shows the prepared footprint, aggregate base, bedding layer and the transition between levels. These are not glamorous stages, but they establish the surface the finished patio depends on.</p>
  <figure class="article-photo article-photo-portrait"><img src="/img/projects/paver-patio-in-progress/base-preparation.jpg" alt="Prepared aggregate base, bedding layer and low wall during a residential patio installation" width="1200" height="1600" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>Base preparation and grade transitions remain visible before the paver field closes the surface.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>Every site has its own drainage, soil, access and elevation constraints. A sound installation responds to those conditions rather than relying on one generic depth or detail. The important homeowner question is not simply what paver is being used, but how the entire assembly and surrounding grades are being resolved.</p>

  <h2 id="setting">Setting the pavers</h2>
  <p>As the field moves forward, long levels and layout tools help control the surface while pieces are measured and cut at the edges. The multi-size pattern is distributed across the space so joints do not fall into distracting lines or small awkward clusters.</p>
  <aside class="article-note"><strong>Why the in-progress view matters</strong><p>A finished surface can hide the preparation beneath it. Progress documentation gives homeowners a useful record of the layout, material transitions and construction stages that will no longer be visible later.</p></aside>

  <h2 id="edges">Connecting hardscape and planting</h2>
  <p>Along the house, a low stone wall defines a planted bed and manages a modest elevation change next to the existing walk. The wall’s stepped profile follows the site instead of forcing one tall, abrupt line.</p>
  <figure class="article-photo article-photo-portrait"><img src="/img/projects/paver-patio-in-progress/planting-wall.jpg" alt="Low stone planting wall beside a residential walk and freshly mulched bed" width="1200" height="1600" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>A low stepped wall gives the planting bed a deliberate edge while responding to the changing grade.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>This is where landscape and hardscape should stop feeling like separate trades. Wall height, bed depth, paving edges, runoff and plant space all affect one another. Resolving them together makes the finished project feel settled into the property.</p>

  <h2 id="next">What comes next</h2>
  <p>The project is not being presented as complete. The remaining work includes finishing the paver field and joints, resolving the open edges, cleaning the work area and completing the surrounding landscape details. Those final steps will make the composition easier to read, but they are only successful because the underlying work came first.</p>
  <p>We will update this field note with finished photographs so the full sequence—from exposed base to completed outdoor room—lives in one place.</p>

